1. **[REPORT §5](/home/knielsen/code/factoring-connection/REPORT.md:419): the generic-model question is not well-posed as stated.** With public binary \(N\), ordinary polynomial-time control, and no unknown input residue, every generated handle can be tracked by its canonical value modulo \(N\). Ring operations, equality and gcd tests can therefore be simulated in polynomial time. Conversely, an ordinary sum-of-two-squares decision algorithm can ignore the ring oracle. Thus “generic ε-decision” under this interpretation is simply ordinary ε-decision, whose factoring equivalence remains unresolved. With a fixed modulus and nonuniform programs, the answer can instead be hardcoded. Hiding \(N\), or restricting the controller, defines a different model that must be specified. There is no information-theoretic impossibility: ε is a ring invariant. **Fix:** formalize uniformity, public inputs, sampling and permitted control before proposing a theorem; the variable-input [Jager–Schwenk result](https://eprint.iacr.org/2009/621.pdf) does not resolve this modulus-only problem.

2. **[REPORT §5](/home/knielsen/code/factoring-connection/REPORT.md:426): “closes the whole \(R_N\)-algebra program … tracks should be retired” does not follow.** Factoring-equivalence transfers algorithms and hardness assumptions; it does not rule out a polynomial-time factoring algorithm—the project’s objective. Nor does it establish that every useful partial-information format must compute ε. **Fix:** replace this conclusion with the precise conditional consequence of the proposed reduction.

3. **[NEXT_STEPS §2](/home/knielsen/code/factoring-connection/NEXT_STEPS.md:27): the proposed mod-64 experiment is algebraically vacuous.** For \(N\equiv1\pmod8\), put \(b=[2\text{ is nonsquare mod }N]\). Then
   \[
   uv\bmod4=b\bigl(1+2[N\equiv1\pmod{16}]\bigr).
   \]
   Indeed, \(b=0\) makes both \(u,v\) even; \(b=1\) makes both odd, and \(N\bmod16\) fixes their sum modulo 4, hence their product modulo 4. Consequently \(N\bmod64,\epsilon,QR_2\) already determine \(s\bmod64\), without either class-group rank. I checked this on all 3,418 stated pairs. **Fix:** investigate \(s\bmod128\) instead. Also delete \(\mathrm{Cl}(-N)\) from the proposed discriminants: here \(-N\equiv3\pmod4\), so it is not a quadratic-order discriminant.

4. **[REPORT §1.1](/home/knielsen/code/factoring-connection/REPORT.md:47): bit positions, moduli and normalized corrections are conflated.** Modulo 8 exposes the weight-\(4\) bit; modulo 16 adds the weight-\(8\) bit; modulo 32 adds weight \(16\). Moreover, the raw new bit is not universally the stated character indicator: it includes the known baseline \(\epsilon(N+1)\). For \(N\equiv5\pmod8\), write the actual congruence
   \[
   s\equiv\epsilon(N+1)-32\epsilon
   \left(\frac{r-\epsilon}{8}\bmod2\right)\pmod{64}.
   \]
   For example, \(p=37,q=41\) gives indicator \(1\), but \(s=78\) has weight-\(32\) bit \(0\). **Fix:** use explicit moduli and describe the indicator as the correction relative to the baseline.

5. **[REPORT rank-1 item](/home/knielsen/code/factoring-connection/REPORT.md:28): “one-bit missing primitive” materially understates the missing operation.** ε and \(QR_2\) supply only constantly many bits; the decoder needs a growing prefix of roughly \(n/4\) bits. No reduction obtains that prefix from either decision. **Fix:** call this a “partial-information interface with an unimplemented growing family of predicates.” Also define “information floor” relative to \(N\bmod m\); it is not an information-theoretic lower bound conditional on the full integer \(N\), which uniquely determines its factors.

6. **[REPORT rank-1 item](/home/knielsen/code/factoring-connection/REPORT.md:28): the threshold is incorrectly labelled “proved.”** The formula containing \(-2\) is an empirical fit, with instance variation; the notes separately give a rigorous sufficient inequality. **Fix:** label the fitted threshold “experimentally supported,” and attach the factoring guarantee to `rigorous_ok` and the exact root-coset statement. Finite observations also do not establish a universal “success iff” threshold.

7. **[NEXT_STEPS §1](/home/knielsen/code/factoring-connection/NEXT_STEPS.md:18): the equivalence’s refutation criterion is invalid.** A decision algorithm that itself never outputs a factor can still enable factoring through a reduction. Comparing its own splitting probability with its prediction advantage cannot refute equivalence. Advantage also needs specified distributions and a baseline: an explicit distribution supported on \(p,q\equiv3\pmod4\) admits a perfect constant predictor. **Fix:** distinguish a counterexample to a particular extraction lemma from a separation of computational problems, and specify balanced challenge distributions and reduction access.

8. **[REPORT §5](/home/knielsen/code/factoring-connection/REPORT.md:420): “contains every mechanism this project built” contradicts the notes’ model audit.** Arbitrary computation modulo \(N^2\), followed by division by \(N\), is not computation in an \(R_N\)-algebra: \(N\cdot1\) vanishes in the latter and not in the former. Integer-representative operations and lattice decoding also require additional access. **Fix:** enumerate which implementations fit the model and which lift formulas have actually been compiled into permitted operations.

9. **[REPORT correlation battery](/home/knielsen/code/factoring-connection/REPORT.md:439): “expected outcome: no signal” overlooks a classical signal already in the proposed menu.** Odd continued-fraction period of \(\sqrt N\) implies solvability of \(x^2-Ny^2=-1\), hence ε\(=+1\). This is an established one-sided connection, not necessarily leakage. Full period computation also lacks the claimed polynomial cost guarantee. **Fix:** distinguish complete periods from bounded prefixes, include this positive control, benchmark costs, and report statistical power. Failure to reach \(5\sigma\) only excludes effects detectable by that experiment; it does not refute all correlation. See the [negative Pell discussion](https://www.math.toronto.edu/~eknight/Pell.pdf).

10. **[REPORT §1.3](/home/knielsen/code/factoring-connection/REPORT.md:111): “The only compressing exponent” is false without the large-instance or threshold qualification.** The notes themselves give non-character compression at smaller primes. I reproduced
    \[
    p=257,\quad m=8,\quad E=70,\quad v=186,
    \]
    which is exact with \(d=7<9=\lceil L/2\rceil\), although \(256\nmid2(E-1)\). **Fix:** say “all observed passes of \(d\le3L/8\) used the character exponent,” or explicitly restrict to the 16 largest instances. Likewise, \(3/4\) “at best” remains an empirical/conjectural asymptotic statement.

11. **[NEXT_STEPS §5](/home/knielsen/code/factoring-connection/NEXT_STEPS.md:81): one finite counterexample cannot refute C2R-X as quantified.** Its \(p_0\) and \(K\) are existential and unspecified; an example above 4099 can be absorbed by increasing either. **Fix:** describe that criterion as refuting the stronger finite-range rule. Refuting C2R-X requires, for some fixed positive ε, an unbounded family of exact folds with linear pair counts and
    \[
    \frac{p-1}{\gcd(p-1,E-1)}\longrightarrow\infty.
    \]

12. **[REPORT §1.2](/home/knielsen/code/factoring-connection/REPORT.md:90): the descent statement omits essential qualifications.** Descending through \((1+X)^N\) can select index \(0\) or \(N\), whose gcd is not proper. **Fix:** subtract the endpoint sections and descend through \(F_N\), as in the brief. Specify that the uniform constructor supports the child queries needed by the descent; existence of short circuits along an unidentified good path is insufficient. Also qualify “sections … are ≤3-sparse” by \(M>p\); C2 does not give sparsity at every level.

13. **[REPORT opening cost claim](/home/knielsen/code/factoring-connection/REPORT.md:22): measured scaling is repeatedly presented as an asymptotic bound.** In particular, “\(\Theta(1/p)\) per attempt” is not a uniform per-instance statement: the report itself records \(P_{\rm gap}=0\) on 65–86% of tested balanced instances. Finite timing slopes likewise do not prove asymptotic lower bounds. **Fix:** separate exact operation counts, empirical distribution averages, heuristic extrapolations and proved restricted-model bounds; use the corresponding status labels.

14. **[REPORT §2.1](/home/knielsen/code/factoring-connection/REPORT.md:153): “the general SLP version … is equivalent to open problems” overstates the cited implications.** The notes describe several differently quantified implications involving factorial multiples, root-rich polynomials, nonuniform circuits and constant-free permanent complexity—not a proved equivalence with the project’s uniform modular predicate. **Fix:** state the individual implications with their hypotheses, and replace “an unconditional obstruction … is out of reach” with “would require substantially stronger lower-bound results than those established here.”